I mean, in 1962 and 1965, in the Stormont Parliament, they won nine out of 52 seats. I mean, they're barely even in opposition, really. And they are generally seen as a bit old-fashioned, very Catholic, very rural, not terribly effective. Kind of Devalera type. A little bit, yeah, but Devalera is a very wily and ruthless politician and they are not. Right.
我的意思是，在1962年和1965年，在斯托蒙特议会中，他们赢得了52个席位中的9个。我的意思是，他们几乎算不上反对派。而且他们通常被认为有点老派，非常天主教，非常乡村，不是特别有效。有点像德瓦莱拉（Devalera）那种类型。有点像，是的，但德瓦莱拉是一个非常狡猾和无情的政治家，而他们不是。对。

68
00:37:01.000 --> 00:37:31.000
Then you have the IRA. Now, the unionist hardliners talk all the time about the IRA. You know, they're obsessed with the IRA. They say the IRA, you know, is always out there, which, I mean, to be fair, it kind of is. The IRA had rebuilt after the Second World War and it had launched a new guerrilla campaign along the border in 1956 called Operation Harvest. and this campaign had been a complete and utter disaster. Both Belfast and Dublin, in fact, Amon de Valera,
然后你有爱尔兰共和军（IRA）。现在，统一派强硬派一直在谈论爱尔兰共和军。你知道，他们对爱尔兰共和军着迷。他们说爱尔兰共和军总是潜伏在那里，说实话，这在某种程度上确实如此。爱尔兰共和军在二战后进行了重建，并于1956年在边境发起了一场新的游击战，称为“丰收行动”（Operation Harvest）。这场运动彻底失败了。贝尔法斯特和都柏林，事实上，埃蒙·德瓦莱拉（Amon de Valera），

69
00:37:31.000 --> 00:37:55.000
reintroduced internment without trial. They interned loads of IRA people and they completely broke the back of the organisation. And so in 1962, the IRA had called off this campaign, they dumped their weapons and madly, a few years later, they sold a lot of their weapons to the Welsh. Oh, to the Free Wales Army. Great. So people thinking it's just about Ireland.
重新引入了未经审判的拘留。他们拘留了大量的爱尔兰共和军成员，彻底摧毁了该组织的骨干。所以到了1962年，爱尔兰共和军取消了这场运动，他们丢弃了武器，疯狂的是，几年后，他们把很多武器卖给了威尔士人。哦，卖给了“自由威尔士军”（Free Wales Army）。太棒了。所以人们以为这只是关于爱尔兰的事。

70
00:37:55.000 --> 00:38:06.000
It's about Wales as well. There's actually too much Welsh history. So much Welsh history in the West of history. It just keeps going. I know. They're everywhere. It's unbelievable. Right.
这也是关于威尔士的事。实际上有太多的威尔士历史。历史的西边有太多的威尔士历史。它一直在继续。我知道。他们无处不在。这简直令人难以置信。对。

71
00:38:06.000 --> 00:38:38.000
So the IRA gets a new chief of staff who's from Dublin called Catholic Goulding. And Goulding says, listen, we have been wasting our time here. This has been a terrible mistake. And you said later, the notion that the IRA was going to rise up someday in Free Ireland and get rid of the British was a ridiculous pipe dream. I mean, that's a hell of a thing for the guy running the IRA to say in the early 60s. And this bloke Goulding, he's actually very close to some Marxist academics and to people from the British Communist Party.
所以爱尔兰共和军迎来了一位来自都柏林的新参谋长，名叫凯瑟琳·古尔丁（Cathal Goulding）。古尔丁说，听着，我们在这里浪费时间了。这是一个可怕的错误。你后来也说过，爱尔兰共和军有一天会在自由爱尔兰起义并赶走英国人的想法是一个荒谬的白日梦。我的意思是，对于一个在60年代初领导爱尔兰共和军的人来说，这真是一句惊人的话。而这个叫古尔丁的家伙，他实际上与一些马克思主义学者和英国共产党的人非常亲近。

72
00:38:38.000 --> 00:39:12.000
And they persuade him the IRA's future lies in socialist activism. So he's listening to the British. He's listened to British academics, I know, which is mad. Never listened to a British academic. Instead of like blowing up post offices and stuff, why don't we work to build a non-sectarian workers' alliance that will fight both imperialism and capitalism? And the people who are running the IRA in the 60s say, yeah, actually, brilliant, let's do that. Because of course that goes with the momentum of the 60s, right? It feels right. Yeah, of course. So do they start going to conferences in goldsmiths?
他们说服他，爱尔兰共和军的未来在于社会主义激进主义。所以他在听英国人的话。他在听英国学者的建议，我知道，这太疯狂了。永远不要听英国学者的建议。与其炸毁邮局之类的，为什么我们不努力建立一个非宗派的工人联盟，既反对帝国主义又反对资本主义呢？而在60年代运营爱尔兰共和军的人说，是的，实际上，太棒了，我们这样做吧。因为这当然符合60年代的势头，对吧？感觉是对的。是的，当然。所以他们开始去金史密斯学院（Goldsmiths）参加会议了吗？

73
00:39:12.000 --> 00:39:54.000
They do. There's probably men with polo necks. They have a lot of seminars. There are a lot of. There's a lot of talking shops about smashing capitalism. They're massively into this. Now, if you're an older IRA veteran, you are not pleased about this at all. So a lot of older IRA men are culturally conservative Catholics. They are not Marxists. So Joe Cahill, who was an IRA veteran who had been jailed for killing a policeman in 1942. He said, the movement was being led off the true path of republicanism. Ultra left politics were taking over. Sooner or later, they would have to be a showdown. And he's right. And the will. be a showdown. I mean, people say that Sinn Féin are doing the same at the moment, that they're becoming Corbinist, really.
他们去了。那里可能有穿着高领毛衣的男人。他们有很多研讨会。有很多关于粉碎资本主义的空谈。他们非常热衷于此。现在，如果你是一个老派的爱尔兰共和军老兵，你对此一点也不高兴。所以很多老派的爱尔兰共和军成员是文化保守的天主教徒。他们不是马克思主义者。所以乔·卡希尔（Joe Cahill），一位因在1942年杀害一名警察而入狱的爱尔兰共和军老兵，他说，运动正在被带离共和主义的真正道路。极左政治正在接管。迟早会有一场摊牌。他是对的。而且确实会有一场摊牌。我的意思是，人们说新芬党（Sinn Féin）现在也在做同样的事情，他们真的变得像科尔宾（Corbynist）了。

74
00:39:54.000 --> 00:40:54.000
Exactly, that they're sort of caught between, you know, the Catholic nationalism on the one hand and a slightly woolly Marxist internationalism. Yes, on the other. Now, we'll come back to this later in the series, but the point really is that they've gone very, very quiet. So a future member of the provisional IRA, Billy McKee's quote from him, I believe that it was dead, completely dead, and it was, except in the minds of myself and other old republic. So, to return to the original question, if you're a Catholic, where are you looking for help? There is an alternative, of course, you could look to Dublin. Now, in theory, the Republic of Ireland claims the whole island. Article 2 of the Irish Constitution, the national territory consists of the whole island of Ireland. And Irish politicians would always say if they're asked, well, our goal, of course, is unification of the entire island. in reality, since the 1920s, governments in Dublin have shown no interest in advancing this at all and done absolutely nothing about it.
完全正确，他们一方面被困在天主教民族主义中，另一方面被困在一种稍微模糊的马克思主义国际主义中。是的，另一方面。现在，我们稍后会在本系列中回到这一点，但重点是他们已经变得非常非常安静了。所以未来临时爱尔兰共和军的一名成员，比利·麦基（Billy McKee）引用他的话说，我相信它已经死了，彻底死了，除了在我自己和其他老共和党人的脑海中。所以，回到最初的问题，如果你是天主教徒，你在哪里寻找帮助？当然有另一种选择，你可以看向都柏林。现在，理论上，爱尔兰共和国声称拥有整个岛屿。《爱尔兰宪法》第2条规定，国家领土由爱尔兰全岛组成。如果被问及，爱尔兰政治家总是会说，嗯，我们的目标当然是整个岛屿的统一。实际上，自20世纪20年代以来，都柏林的政府对推进这一点毫无兴趣，也完全没有采取任何行动。

75
00:40:54.000 --> 00:41:27.000
And again, this is something that will surprise, I think, a lot of listeners. Twice in the Second World War, the British said to the government in Dublin, we will give you a united Ireland if you abandon your neutrality and join the Allies. after Pearl Harbor in 1941, Winston Churchill sent an incredible telegram to Amon de Valera. Now is your chance, now or never, a nation once again. So the nation once again is a kind of Republican slogan. Churchill was using it. And Devala just completely ignored him.
而且，我认为这会让很多听众感到惊讶。在二战期间，英国人曾两次对都柏林政府说，如果你放弃中立并加入盟军，我们将给你一个统一的爱尔兰。1941年珍珠港事件后，温斯顿·丘吉尔（Winston Churchill）给埃蒙·德瓦莱拉发了一封令人难以置信的电报。现在是你的机会，机不可失，时不再来，国家再次统一。所以“国家再次统一”是一种共和主义口号。丘吉尔当时正在使用它。而德瓦莱拉完全无视了他。

76
00:41:27.000 --> 00:42:04.000
Didn't want to abandon neutrality. And also, of course, if you are a politician in Dublin, you don't really want those one million Protestants who have a tendency to sign governance and join volunteer, groups to fight you. I mean, that's an issue right the way up to the present, really, isn't it? To the present, exactly, of course. So in 1959, Devalera, the great champion of Irish independence and Irish republicanism, retired as Taoiseach, and he became president of Ireland, kind of kicked upstairs, and he was succeeded by a man called Sean Lamas. I mean, it's unbelievable, he's called Le Mass. I know, I know. I mean, anything more calculated to
不想放弃中立。而且，当然，如果你是都柏林的政治家，你并不真的想要那一百万有倾向签署治理协议并加入志愿组织来对抗你的新教徒。我的意思是，这确实是一个一直持续到今天的问题，不是吗？直到今天，完全正确，当然。所以1959年，爱尔兰独立和爱尔兰共和主义的伟大捍卫者德瓦莱拉退休了，不再担任总理（Taoiseach），他成为了爱尔兰总统，算是被“提拔”了，接替他的是一个叫肖恩·勒马斯（Sean Lemass）的人。我的意思是，这令人难以置信，他叫勒马斯。我知道，我知道。我的意思是，还有什么比勒马斯更让佩斯利感到……

77
00:42:04.000 --> 00:42:26.000
alami and Paisley than LaMas. We shall come to this. LaMass had fought in the Easter Rising and he'd also been in the IRA. But in private, Lamass said to people, come on. The island's going to be partitioned for a long time yet. There's no point in whinging about this. It's not going to change anytime soon. So Dublin's not really interested in the Catholics in Northern Ireland.
比勒马斯更让佩斯利感到……我们稍后会谈到这一点。勒马斯参加过复活节起义，也参加过爱尔兰共和军。但在私下里，勒马斯对人们说，得了吧。这个岛还要被分裂很长一段时间。抱怨这个没有意义。它不会很快改变。所以都柏林对北爱尔兰的天主教徒并不真正感兴趣。

78
00:42:26.000 --> 00:43:00.000
And if they're not really interested, people who are even less interested are politicians in London. Now, there are some Labor MPs. There are always Labour MPs who campaign against discrimination against the Catholics. So this goes back to your point on how could this happen in Britain? Well, I kind of think, I mean, it's a massive block on the British state that it's allowing this to happen, I think. But the British state, first of all, it's running an empire for most of this period. So, I mean, it's not like there are other places where there are, you know, interesting local arrangements.
如果他们不感兴趣，那么伦敦的政治家就更不感兴趣了。现在，有一些工党议员。总有一些工党议员反对对天主教徒的歧视。所以这回到了你关于这怎么可能在英国发生的观点上。嗯，我有点认为，我的意思是，英国国家允许这种情况发生是一个巨大的污点，我想。但英国国家，首先，在这一时期的大部分时间里，它在运营一个帝国。所以，我的意思是，这并不是说没有其他地方有，你知道，有趣的当地安排。

79
00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:35.000
But by the mid-60s. Yes, and people in the 60s start to say this. So you have a Labour backbench, what's it called, the campaign for democracy in Ulster, that kicks off in the mid-60s. You have newspapers, like the Times, the Sunday Times, the Guardian, drawing attention to this and saying it's kind of not right that these people don't get houses and they don't really get to vote in local elections and stuff like this. But here's why people don't get massively excited about it. ever since the 1920s, most British politicians have worked in the assumption of the Irish question which had dogged Britain for so long has been solved. It's done.
但到了60年代中期。是的，60年代的人们开始这么说。所以你有一个工党后座议员组织，叫什么来着，阿尔斯特民主运动（campaign for democracy in Ulster），它在60年代中期开始。你有像《泰晤士报》、《星期日泰晤士报》、《卫报》这样的报纸，引起人们对这一点的关注，并说这些人得不到房子，在地方选举中也无法真正投票，这有点不对劲。但这就是为什么人们对此并不感到非常兴奋的原因：自20世纪20年代以来，大多数英国政治家都认为困扰英国已久的爱尔兰问题已经解决了。它完成了。

80
00:43:35.000 --> 00:43:58.000
It's finished. The people in the North, we've fixed it because they've got their own parliament. We'll just let them get on with it. It's not perfect. But we absolutely don't want to interfere because they have a tendency to start arming themselves and we don't want to stir them up again. What has happened in Britain is that Northern Ireland has vanished from the national consciousness. It virtually never appears in the newspapers.
它结束了。北方的人们，我们已经解决了，因为他们有自己的议会。我们让他们自己处理吧。这并不完美。但我们绝对不想干涉，因为他们有武装自己的倾向，我们不想再次煽动他们。在英国发生的事情是，北爱尔兰已经从民族意识中消失了。它几乎从未出现在报纸上。

81
00:43:58.000 --> 00:44:17.000
It's never mentioned in TV bulletins, news bulletins. Most people never think about it at all. I mentioned Jim Callahan before. Jim Callahan, grandson of Irish immigrant. And I quote, most members of Parliament knew less about it than we knew about our distant colonies on the far side of the earth.
它从未在电视新闻简报中被提及。大多数人根本从不考虑它。我之前提到过吉姆·卡拉汉（Jim Callahan）。吉姆·卡拉汉，爱尔兰移民的孙子。我引用他的话，大多数国会议员对它的了解比我们对地球另一端遥远殖民地的了解还要少。

82
00:44:17.000 --> 00:44:32.000
And I think that's true. There's loads in British newspapers about Cyprus in the 1950s. There's loads about Kenya. There's loads about Malaysia. there's virtually nothing about Northern Ireland. And actually, people think, well, what's the point? Nothing's ever going to change.
我认为这是真的。英国报纸上有很多关于1950年代塞浦路斯的内容。有很多关于肯尼亚的内容。有很多关于马来西亚的内容。几乎没有关于北爱尔兰的内容。实际上，人们想，嗯，有什么意义呢？什么都不会改变。

83
00:44:32.000 --> 00:44:52.000
The unionists hold every single Westminster seat. The nationalist is totally irrelevant. The IRA have been beaten. And frankly, most people in Northern Ireland, they might be Protestant, they might be Catholic, but this is the 60s. It's the affluent society. They're more interested in buying a new TV or a new washing machine than they are about refighting the kind of the battles of the past.
统一派占据了每一个威斯敏斯特席位。民族主义者完全无关紧要。爱尔兰共和军已经被击败了。坦率地说，北爱尔兰的大多数人，他们可能是新教徒，也可能是天主教徒，但这是60年代。这是一个富裕的社会。他们对购买新电视或新洗衣机比对重打过去的战争更感兴趣。

84
00:44:52.000 --> 00:45:33.000
So this is the Sandbrook perspective. Well, that would be the Sanbrough, perspective and I would be wrong all about washing machines. In this instance, my washing machine eccentric view of history would be absolutely discredited because everybody thinks life is going to go on, everything is going to be the same forever and ever and they are wrong because things are about to change very dramatically indeed. And Dominic, we will find out how and why after a break. There have been disappointments. frustrations, but progress has been so marked that there is no excuse for pessimism about the future.
所以这就是桑布鲁克（Sandbrook）的视角。嗯，那将是桑布鲁克的视角，而我关于洗衣机的观点将是完全错误的。在这种情况下，我那种关于洗衣机的古怪历史观将被彻底否定，因为每个人都认为生活会继续下去，一切都会永远保持不变，但他们错了，因为事情即将发生非常剧烈的变化。多米尼克，我们将在休息后找出原因和方式。虽然有失望，有挫折，但进步是如此显著，以至于没有理由对未来感到悲观。

85
00:45:33.000 --> 00:46:04.000
The six counties, unlike their neighbours across the border, belong to the British welfare state. They share too, as they richly deserve, many of the fruits of the affluent society. The processes of modernisation have been carried far, and they are not halted. On the contrary, they are on the march. So welcome back to the rest of history and that ringingly optimistic, groovy 60s perspective was in the Times
这六个郡，与边境那边的邻居不同，属于英国福利国家。他们也分享了，正如他们理应得到的那样，富裕社会的许多成果。现代化的进程已经走得很远，而且没有停止。相反，它们正在前进。欢迎回到历史的其余部分，那种响亮而乐观、时髦的60年代视角出现在《泰晤士报》上。

86
00:46:04.000 --> 00:46:17.000
on the 12th of April, in 1965, looking ahead to a bright new future for Northern Ireland. Great to see the Times with its finger on the pulse. Always. The paper for top people.
1965年4月12日，展望北爱尔兰光明的未来。很高兴看到《泰晤士报》紧跟时代脉搏。一直如此。顶级人士的报纸。

87
00:46:17.000 --> 00:46:42.000
And I suppose that top people sitting in their gentleman's clubs reading those words, it would have seen beyond the bounds of possibility to them, that within a few years, there would be bombs going off everywhere in the streets of Belfast and Derry, British soldiers patrolling those streets, hundreds of people being killed every year, and the carnage of it all spreading to the British mainland. Yeah.
我想，那些坐在绅士俱乐部里读着这些话的顶级人士，在他们看来，几年之内，贝尔法斯特和德里的街道上到处都会发生爆炸，英国士兵在街上巡逻，每年有数百人被杀，而这一切的杀戮蔓延到英国本土，这在他们看来是不可能的。是的。

88
00:46:42.000 --> 00:47:01.000
So what goes wrong? I mean, what happens? What happens? But big question. That's what the series is all about, Tom. So let's start on the 25th of March, 1963. So this is the day that Captain Terence O'Neill becomes the fourth Prime Minister of Northern Ireland. So in 1963, Harold McMillan is Prime Minister in Britain.
那么出了什么问题？我的意思是，发生了什么？发生了什么？这是一个大问题。这就是本系列节目的全部内容，汤姆。所以让我们从1963年3月25日开始。这是特伦斯·奥尼尔（Terence O'Neill）上尉成为北爱尔兰第四任总理的日子。所以1963年，哈罗德·麦克米伦（Harold McMillan）是英国首相。

